2517:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****     /*
2518:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * When min == 0, each try has at worst a probability 1/2 of failing
2519:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * (the msb has a probability 1/2 of being 0, and then the result will
2520:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * be < N), so after 30 tries failure probability is a most 2**(-30).
2521:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      *
2522:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * When N is just below a power of 2, as is the case when generating
2523:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * a random scalar on most elliptic curves, 1 try is enough with
2524:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * overwhelming probability. When N is just above a power of 2,
2525:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * as when generating a random scalar on secp224k1, each try has
2526:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * a probability of failing that is almost 1/2.
ARM GAS  C:\Users\nicx5\AppData\Local\Temp\ccMLdj3f.s 			page 232


2527:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      *
2528:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * The probabilities are almost the same if min is nonzero but negligible
2529:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * compared to N. This is always the case when N is crypto-sized, but
2530:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * it's convenient to support small N for testing purposes. When N
2531:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * is small, use a higher repeat count, otherwise the probability of
2532:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * failure is macroscopic.
2533:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      */
2534:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****     count = (n_bytes > 4 ? 30 : 250);
